{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba ../native-source/transport.go | sed -n '109,140p'\nnl -ba ../native-source/replication.go | sed -n '229,263p;534,566p'\nnl -ba ../native-source/inmem_transport.go | sed -n '259,374p'\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr=json.load(open('../research.json'))\nfor k in ['claims','bindings']: print(k,json.dumps(r[k],indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"   109\t// AppendPipeline is used for pipelining AppendEntries requests. It is used\n   110\t// to increase the replication throughput by masking latency and better\n   111\t// utilizing bandwidth.\n   112\ttype AppendPipeline interface {\n   113\t\t// AppendEntries is used to add another request to the pipeline.\n   114\t\t// The send may block which is an effective form of back-pressure.\n   115\t\tAppendEntries(args *AppendEntriesRequest, resp *AppendEntriesResponse) (AppendFuture, error)\n   116\t\n   117\t\t// Consumer returns a channel that can be used to consume\n   118\t\t// response futures when they are ready.\n   119\t\tConsumer() <-chan AppendFuture\n   120\t\n   121\t\t// Close closes the pipeline and cancels all inflight RPCs\n   122\t\tClose() error\n   123\t}\n   124\t\n   125\t// AppendFuture is used to return information about a pipelined AppendEntries request.\n   126\ttype AppendFuture interface {\n   127\t\tFuture\n   128\t\n   129\t\t// Start returns the time that the append request was started.\n   130\t\t// It is always OK to call this method.\n   131\t\tStart() time.Time\n   132\t\n   133\t\t// Request holds the parameters of the AppendEntries call.\n   134\t\t// It is always OK to call this method.\n   135\t\tRequest() *AppendEntriesRequest\n   136\t\n   137\t\t// Response holds the results of the AppendEntries call.\n   138\t\t// This method must only be called after the Error\n   139\t\t// method returns, and will only be valid on success.\n   140\t\tResponse() *AppendEntriesResponse\n   229\t\t// Make the RPC call\n   230\t\tstart = time.Now()\n   231\t\tif err := r.trans.AppendEntries(peer.ID, peer.Address, &req, &resp); err != nil {\n   232\t\t\tr.logger.Error(\"failed to appendEntries to\", \"peer\", peer, \"error\", err)\n   233\t\t\ts.failures++\n   234\t\t\treturn\n   235\t\t}\n   236\t\tappendStats(string(peer.ID), start, float32(len(req.Entries)), r.noLegacyTelemetry)\n   237\t\n   238\t\t// Check for a newer term, stop running\n   239\t\tif resp.Term > req.Term {\n   240\t\t\tr.handleStaleTerm(s)\n   241\t\t\treturn true\n   242\t\t}\n   243\t\n   244\t\t// Update the last contact\n   245\t\ts.setLastContact()\n   246\t\n   247\t\t// Update s based on success\n   248\t\tif resp.Success {\n   249\t\t\t// Update our replication state\n   250\t\t\tupdateLastAppended(s, &req)\n   251\t\n   252\t\t\t// Clear any failures, allow pipelining\n   253\t\t\ts.failures = 0\n   254\t\t\ts.allowPipeline = true\n   255\t\t} else {\n   256\t\t\tatomic.StoreUint64(&s.nextIndex, max(min(s.nextIndex-1, resp.LastLog+1), 1))\n   257\t\t\tif resp.NoRetryBackoff {\n   258\t\t\t\ts.failures = 0\n   259\t\t\t} else {\n   260\t\t\t\ts.failures++\n   261\t\t\t}\n   262\t\t\tr.logger.Warn(\"appendEntries rejected, sending older logs\", \"peer\", peer, \"next\", atomic.LoadUint64(&s.nextIndex))\n   263\t\t}\n   534\tfunc (r *Raft) pipelineDecode(s *followerReplication, p AppendPipeline, stopCh, finishCh chan struct{}) {\n   535\t\tdefer close(finishCh)\n   536\t\trespCh := p.Consumer()\n   537\t\tfor {\n   538\t\t\tselect {\n   539\t\t\tcase ready := <-respCh:\n   540\t\t\t\ts.peerLock.RLock()\n   541\t\t\t\tpeer := s.peer\n   542\t\t\t\ts.peerLock.RUnlock()\n   543\t\n   544\t\t\t\treq, resp := ready.Request(), ready.Response()\n   545\t\t\t\tappendStats(string(peer.ID), ready.Start(), float32(len(req.Entries)), r.noLegacyTelemetry)\n   546\t\n   547\t\t\t\t// Check for a newer term, stop running\n   548\t\t\t\tif resp.Term > req.Term {\n   549\t\t\t\t\tr.handleStaleTerm(s)\n   550\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   551\t\t\t\t}\n   552\t\n   553\t\t\t\t// Update the last contact\n   554\t\t\t\ts.setLastContact()\n   555\t\n   556\t\t\t\t// Abort pipeline if not successful\n   557\t\t\t\tif !resp.Success {\n   558\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   559\t\t\t\t}\n   560\t\n   561\t\t\t\t// Update our replication state\n   562\t\t\t\tupdateLastAppended(s, req)\n   563\t\t\tcase <-stopCh:\n   564\t\t\t\treturn\n   565\t\t\t}\n   566\t\t}\n   259\tfunc newInmemPipeline(trans *InmemTransport, peer *InmemTransport, addr ServerAddress) *inmemPipeline {\n   260\t\ti := &inmemPipeline{\n   261\t\t\ttrans:        trans,\n   262\t\t\tpeer:         peer,\n   263\t\t\tpeerAddr:     addr,\n   264\t\t\tdoneCh:       make(chan AppendFuture, 16),\n   265\t\t\tinprogressCh: make(chan *inmemPipelineInflight, 16),\n   266\t\t\tshutdownCh:   make(chan struct{}),\n   267\t\t}\n   268\t\tgo i.decodeResponses()\n   269\t\treturn i\n   270\t}\n   271\t\n   272\tfunc (i *inmemPipeline) decodeResponses() {\n   273\t\ttimeout := i.trans.timeout\n   274\t\tfor {\n   275\t\t\tselect {\n   276\t\t\tcase inp := <-i.inprogressCh:\n   277\t\t\t\tvar timeoutCh <-chan time.Time\n   278\t\t\t\tif timeout > 0 {\n   279\t\t\t\t\ttimeoutCh = time.After(timeout)\n   280\t\t\t\t}\n   281\t\n   282\t\t\t\tselect {\n   283\t\t\t\tcase rpcResp := <-inp.respCh:\n   284\t\t\t\t\t// Copy the result back\n   285\t\t\t\t\t*inp.future.resp = *rpcResp.Response.(*AppendEntriesResponse)\n   286\t\t\t\t\tinp.future.respond(rpcResp.Error)\n   287\t\n   288\t\t\t\t\tselect {\n   289\t\t\t\t\tcase i.doneCh <- inp.future:\n   290\t\t\t\t\tcase <-i.shutdownCh:\n   291\t\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   292\t\t\t\t\t}\n   293\t\n   294\t\t\t\tcase <-timeoutCh:\n   295\t\t\t\t\tinp.future.respond(fmt.Errorf(\"command timed out\"))\n   296\t\t\t\t\tselect {\n   297\t\t\t\t\tcase i.doneCh <- inp.future:\n   298\t\t\t\t\tcase <-i.shutdownCh:\n   299\t\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   300\t\t\t\t\t}\n   301\t\n   302\t\t\t\tcase <-i.shutdownCh:\n   303\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   304\t\t\t\t}\n   305\t\t\tcase <-i.shutdownCh:\n   306\t\t\t\treturn\n   307\t\t\t}\n   308\t\t}\n   309\t}\n   310\t\n   311\tfunc (i *inmemPipeline) AppendEntries(args *AppendEntriesRequest, resp *AppendEntriesResponse) (AppendFuture, error) {\n   312\t\t// Create a new future\n   313\t\tfuture := &appendFuture{\n   314\t\t\tstart: time.Now(),\n   315\t\t\targs:  args,\n   316\t\t\tresp:  resp,\n   317\t\t}\n   318\t\tfuture.init()\n   319\t\n   320\t\t// Handle a timeout\n   321\t\tvar timeout <-chan time.Time\n   322\t\tif i.trans.timeout > 0 {\n   323\t\t\ttimeout = time.After(i.trans.timeout)\n   324\t\t}\n   325\t\n   326\t\t// Send the RPC over\n   327\t\trespCh := make(chan RPCResponse, 1)\n   328\t\trpc := RPC{\n   329\t\t\tCommand:  args,\n   330\t\t\tRespChan: respCh,\n   331\t\t}\n   332\t\n   333\t\t// Check if we have been already shutdown, otherwise the random choose\n   334\t\t// made by select statement below might pick consumerCh even if\n   335\t\t// shutdownCh was closed.\n   336\t\ti.shutdownLock.RLock()\n   337\t\tshutdown := i.shutdown\n   338\t\ti.shutdownLock.RUnlock()\n   339\t\tif shutdown {\n   340\t\t\treturn nil, ErrPipelineShutdown\n   341\t\t}\n   342\t\n   343\t\tselect {\n   344\t\tcase i.peer.consumerCh <- rpc:\n   345\t\tcase <-timeout:\n   346\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"command enqueue timeout\")\n   347\t\tcase <-i.shutdownCh:\n   348\t\t\treturn nil, ErrPipelineShutdown\n   349\t\t}\n   350\t\n   351\t\t// Send to be decoded\n   352\t\tselect {\n   353\t\tcase i.inprogressCh <- &inmemPipelineInflight{future, respCh}:\n   354\t\t\treturn future, nil\n   355\t\tcase <-i.shutdownCh:\n   356\t\t\treturn nil, ErrPipelineShutdown\n   357\t\t}\n   358\t}\n   359\t\n   360\tfunc (i *inmemPipeline) Consumer() <-chan AppendFuture {\n   361\t\treturn i.doneCh\n   362\t}\n   363\t\n   364\tfunc (i *inmemPipeline) Close() error {\n   365\t\ti.shutdownLock.Lock()\n   366\t\tdefer i.shutdownLock.Unlock()\n   367\t\tif i.shutdown {\n   368\t\t\treturn nil\n   369\t\t}\n   370\t\n   371\t\ti.shutdown = true\n   372\t\tclose(i.shutdownCh)\n   373\t\treturn nil\n   374\t}\nclaims [\n  {\n    \"id\": \"failed-pipeline-response-not-consumed\",\n    \"kind\": \"obligation\",\n    \"description\": \"For a completed AppendFuture consumed by Raft.pipelineDecode whose Error result is non-nil, that consumer must not call Response on that future.\",\n    \"scope\": {\n      \"description\": \"Local failed-future response-validity consumption at the AppendFuture interface, using built-in in-memory timeout production and an isolated pipelineDecode consumer.\",\n      \"assumptions\": [\n        \"A permitted delayed RPC reply may exceed the configured InmemTransport timeout.\",\n        \"The ready-future channel adapter preserves the submitted future identity, completion state and order.\"\n      ],\n      \"excluded\": [\n        \"This check ends at AppendFuture.Response interface access. It does not establish invalid log matching, a false quorum, loss of leader authority, or an unsafe application.\",\n        \"The local consumer is invoked directly with minimal Raft/followerReplication state; election, log persistence and the full replication sender loop are not executed.\",\n        \"The successful control peer is scripted at the RPC interface. Its acknowledgement is not evidence of durable follower storage.\",\n        \"Ready futures are collected from the real InmemTransport pipeline and delivered through a delegating channel adapter in original order. Delayed scheduling after completion is permitted, but a full deployment schedule is not reproduced.\",\n        \"NetworkTransport publishes error-bearing futures too by source reading; this check does not execute that transport or byte decoding.\",\n        \"The existing zero-valued timeout response has Success=false and may prevent match advancement despite invalid interface access. The check must not promote the access result to a commit-safety finding.\"\n      ],\n      \"parameters\": {\n        \"term\": 4,\n        \"request_index\": 2,\n        \"transport_timeout_ms\": 100,\n        \"scenarios\": [\n          \"timeout\",\n          \"success\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"source\": \"Candidate derived from attributed implementation responsibilities\",\n    \"source_ids\": [\n      \"pipeline-interface\",\n      \"future-status-contract\",\n      \"pipeline-result\",\n      \"inmem-pipeline-producer\"\n    ],\n    \"pending\": [],\n    \"assessment\": \"unassessed\",\n    \"candidate\": true,\n    \"grounding\": {\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"pipeline-result\",\n        \"inmem-pipeline-producer\",\n        \"inmem-pipeline-submit\",\n        \"append-future-access\",\n        \"ordinary-result-control\"\n      ],\n      \"expectation_ids\": [\n        \"pipeline-interface\",\n        \"future-status-contract\"\n      ],\n      \"binding_ids\": [\n        \"pipeline-consumer-binding\",\n        \"inmem-producer-binding\"\n      ],\n      \"derivation\": \"AppendFuture.Response explicitly has a successful-Error-return precondition (transport.go:137-140); Future.Error supplies actual completion status. A completed future whose Error returns non-nil therefore has no valid Response to consume. The obligation is the necessary safety condition that a failed future is not accessed via Response, not a requirement to consume every successful future. Receipt from Consumer can establish completion but cannot erase a non-nil result: the built-in inmem producer publishes a timeout future after respond(error), and the network producer likewise publishes after decode errors. This expectation is an interface-use responsibility, distinct from durable-prefix or consensus correctness.\",\n      \"applicability\": \"pipelineDecode receives AppendFuture through AppendPipeline.Consumer and calls its Response method. The selected concrete producer is built-in InmemTransport under a withheld response exceeding its configured timeout; the failure is generated by actual producer code, not assigned by the harness.\",\n      \"unresolved\": [\n        \"This check ends at AppendFuture.Response interface access. It does not establish invalid log matching, a false quorum, loss of leader authority, or an unsafe application.\",\n        \"The local consumer is invoked directly with minimal Raft/followerReplication state; election, log persistence and the full replication sender loop are not executed.\",\n        \"The successful control peer is scripted at the RPC interface. Its acknowledgement is not evidence of durable follower storage.\",\n        \"Ready futures are collected from the real InmemTransport pipeline and delivered through a delegating channel adapter in original order. Delayed scheduling after completion is permitted, but a full deployment schedule is not reproduced.\",\n        \"NetworkTransport publishes error-bearing futures too by source reading; this check does not execute that transport or byte decoding.\",\n        \"The existing zero-valued timeout response has Success=false and may prevent match advancement despite invalid interface access. The check must not promote the access result to a commit-safety finding.\"\n      ],\n      \"conflicts\": [],\n      \"alternatives\": [\n        \"The ready channel synchronizes completion and may eliminate waiting, but does not make an errored response valid.\",\n        \"A zero-valued timeout response and !resp.Success can prevent log progress even if the Response access precondition is violated.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"version\": 1\n  }\n]\nbindings [\n  {\n    \"associations\": [\n      {\n        \"claim_id\": \"failed-pipeline-response-not-consumed\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"pipeline-interface\",\n          \"pipeline-result\"\n        ],\n        \"rationale\": \"Consumer reads Response, then checks term and success; its return closes finishCh.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"anchor\": {\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"pipeline-result\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary_complete\": false,\n      \"kind\": \"declaration\",\n      \"material_id\": \"pipeline-result\",\n      \"start_line\": 534,\n      \"end_line\": 534,\n      \"symbol\": \"pipelineDecode\"\n    },\n    \"material_id\": \"pipeline-result\",\n    \"id\": \"pipeline-consumer-binding\",\n    \"file\": \"replication.go\",\n    \"symbol\": \"pipelineDecode\",\n    \"start_line\": 534,\n    \"end_line\": 566,\n    \"snapshot_id\": \"ff893fa063154958b399a88f2360bfa3\",\n    \"content_digest\": \"24106024107950339bd30fc4e24a0b4a0a97a1c0e01aa7b31bedf4a2ad30b054\",\n    \"basis\": \"agent_inference\",\n    \"description\": \"Consumer reads Response, then checks term and success; its return closes finishCh.\",\n    \"pending\": [],\n    \"version\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"associations\": [\n      {\n        \"claim_id\": \"failed-pipeline-response-not-consumed\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"pipeline-interface\",\n          \"inmem-pipeline-producer\"\n        ],\n        \"rationale\": \"Built-in inmemPipeline completes and publishes normal and timeout futures.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"anchor\": {\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"inmem-pipeline-producer\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary_complete\": true,\n      \"kind\": \"declaration\",\n      \"material_id\": \"inmem-pipeline-producer\",\n      \"start_line\": 272,\n      \"end_line\": 272,\n      \"symbol\": \"decodeResponses\"\n    },\n    \"material_id\": \"inmem-pipeline-producer\",\n    \"id\": \"inmem-producer-binding\",\n    \"file\": \"inmem_transport.go\",\n    \"symbol\": \"decodeResponses\",\n    \"start_line\": 272,\n    \"end_line\": 309,\n    \"snapshot_id\": \"ff893fa063154958b399a88f2360bfa3\",\n    \"content_digest\": \"80d9e25c23cce532a687eafe5197c076d440b5a2be443bc9b3897b4e2fa09774\",\n    \"basis\": \"agent_inference\",\n    \"description\": \"Built-in inmemPipeline completes and publishes normal and timeout futures.\",\n    \"pending\": [],\n    \"version\": 1\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > build_review.py <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nartifact='8e175c8096984a60923b5cce5ff76c89'\nids=['append-future-access','future-status-contract','inmem-pipeline-producer','inmem-pipeline-submit','ordinary-result-control','pipeline-interface','pipeline-result','future-completion']\nrationale='''Whole-artifact correspondence review of direct check 8e175c8096984a60923b5cce5ff76c89 version 1 and execution 183d31024372490eaa400890d8fa2127. I read the archived plan.json and assurance_generated_test.go in direct-checks/64131e59706b4ee1a99de5cab68bafb4, the full stdout.log and stderr.log for that execution, the accepted obligation/question/bindings in research.json, and the required target ranges.\n\nApplicability: pipeline-interface (transport.go:137-140) conditions Response validity on successful completion and requires Error to have returned first. future-status-contract supplies the completion status. pipelineDecode is an actual consumer of this interface, and its Response call immediately feeds operational Term and Success tests (pipeline-result). In this consumer, the obligation concerns consuming a failed future's response, not merely retrieving an opaque pointer for diagnostics. The built-in inmem producer explicitly publishes timeout futures (inmem-pipeline-producer:294-300). Thus channel readiness supplies completedness but is not a substitute for success. Ordinary replication checks transport errors before response use (ordinary-result-control); that is corroborating alternate-path behavior, not the normative basis.\n\nConstruction and legality: the archived harness calls the real InmemTransport constructors, Connect, AppendEntriesPipeline and AppendEntries. It receives the actual peer RPC and verifies request pointer identity. In the timeout case it withholds a reply, causing the built-in producer to complete the real appendFuture with its own timeout error. It verifies that the ready future is the submitted future. This is an executed local producer-to-consumer history, with the channel adapter as a declared substituted delivery edge; it is not an executed election or durable-log history. The successful control uses a scripted RPC reply and the same producer. No target files changed according to the execution record.\n\nObservation and independence: assuranceObservedFuture increments responseCalls only when the target consumer invokes Response and delegates the returned value unchanged. It promotes the real future's other methods. The wrapper is only passed to the consumer; no harness call contributes to its counter. finishCh synchronization precedes reading that counter, avoiding an unsynchronized count observation. Actual ready.Error is read only after consumer completion, supplies future_failed independently of the response counter, and is checked against the intended scenario. The producer fixes this error before publishing the future (future-completion and inmem-pipeline-producer). Neither a predicted failure nor an expected count is emitted as an observation.\n\nCompletion and correlation: admitted and ready are prerequisite events; consumer_completed is a distinct result event. The ready prerequisite references admitted.operation_id, while the property independently associates all observations by operation_id. There is exactly one measured future and one result for each of timeout and success, with pointer checks inside each scenario. The raw log contains all three events in causal order for both IDs. A second independently completed rejection future is queued to terminate the permitted success path; it is not the measured future and its Response calls are not counted. An unreached producer or unfinished consumer would fail the Go test instead of emit a completed result. Both consumers actually finished in the recorded run.\n\nOracle: the saved event_implication is completed AND future_failed implies response_calls == 0. It faithfully checks the scoped necessary condition without requiring success on nonfailed inputs, without treating a result as a prerequisite, and without filtering out the failed case. The raw timeout result is completed=true, future_failed=true, response_calls=1; the independent antecedent occurred and the assertion is false. The success result is completed=true, future_failed=false, response_calls=1 and imposes no failure through this implication. The runner's checker result is violated, consistent with those raw fields. Go tests_passed indicates successful experiment completion, not a passing property.\n\nScope and alternatives: the zero-initialized timeout response has Success=false, and pipelineDecode's rejection branch prevents using that timeout as log support on this path. Both logged commit_index values are 0. This alternative prevents an inference of unsafe commitment, but does not satisfy the failed-response validity precondition itself. The local measured discrepancy therefore remains attributable to the accepted interface-use obligation. No model calibration or global correctness claim follows from this review. No encoding or construction issue was found in this version within its stated endpoint.'''\nlimitations=[\n 'The finding is failed-future response consumption at the local AppendFuture boundary, not an unsafe decision, false quorum, authority violation or application effect.',\n 'Full pipelineReplicate setup, election, sender-loop scheduling and follower log persistence are replaced or absent. The index-2 request is a legal transport input; its occurrence in a valid replicated-log history is not established.',\n 'The scripted success/rejection replies are controls, not evidence about a real follower acknowledgement or durable prefix.',\n 'NetworkTransport decoding, partial-response field mutation and other injected implementations remain unexecuted. A next useful distinct relation is whether the built-in network decoder can publish failed futures with nonzero success fields under permitted truncation; its applicability and consequences require separate source review and observation.',\n 'The checker intentionally does not audit Response access ordering for successful futures, termination in all schedules, or every error case.',\n 'The accepted cross-incarnation object-isolation explanation remains separate; this local result does not reopen it.'\n]\nsubmission={'action':'review','artifact_id':artifact,'rationale':'Complete the mandatory whole-artifact review using the saved artifact and its actual successful experiment run. The observed violation is limited to failed-future response consumption; zero commit indexes and unexecuted wider histories remain explicit limits.','sources':[],\n 'review_items':[{'target_id':artifact,'aspect':'checker_correspondence','status':'no_issue_found','source_ids':ids,'rationale':rationale,'counterevidence':[],'limitations':limitations}],\n 'resolutions':[]}\nPath('submission.json').write_text(json.dumps(submission,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nimport jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(submission,json.load(open('../native-submission.schema.json')))\nprint('Review submission schema validation passed.')\nPY\npython3 build_review.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
